The 3.0 version of the DIS Network Library differs in significant ways from
previous versions.

1) Functionally, the library no longer uses a linked list of incoming PDUs.
It uses two circular buffers, one for incoming PDUs, the other for the
application to read from. When the application exhausts the PDUs in its
buffer, it forces a buffer swap. This method allows the application to
consume PDUs without tying up the network read process, as previous
versions did. The only contention for the controlling semaphore is when the
buffer swap takes place, but this is simply a quick switch of state.

The disadvantages to this approach are that PDUs are lost when the net read
process overwrites its circular buffer by wrapping around to the beginning.
But this must be weighed against the application's inability to keep up
with the rate of incoming PDUs. Under the old method, memory consumption
would continue without check.

2) There has also been change to the application interface to the network
library. Coded examples of the use of the library are in test_it.c, a
sample front end. Compiling guidance is encoded in the makefile. The actual
changes are as follows:

a) old: int net_open(char *interf)
   new:	int net_open(char *interf, int buf_len)

        interf: ptr to ethernet interface, e.g. "et0"
	buf_len: number of PDU unions (array elements) in buffers

a) old: int net_read(char **buf, PDUType *type)
   new:	union PDU *net_read(struct readstat *rstat)

	rstat: struct of read status info

a) old: int net_write(char *pdu, PDUType type)
   new:	no change

a) old: void net_close()
   new:	no change

3) The library is now written in ANSI-compatible C.
